    "title": "Texas coach Sean Miller prepares for Braden Smith's 'greatest gift,' Purdue basketball in Sweet 16",
    "text": "March 24, 2026, 1:06 p.m. ETTexas coach Sean Miller praised Purdue point guard Braden Smith ahead of their teams' Sweet 16 game.Miller highlighted Smith breaking Bobby Hurley's NCAA assist record, which had stood since 1992.The coach described Smith as having incredible vision and making the game easier for his teammates.Texas coach Sean Miller had high praise for the leader of a Purdue basketball team he is scheming to stop.In-between doing so, Miller joined CBS' Jon Rothstein on Inside College Basketball Now to discuss, among a number of things, the 11th-seeded Longhorns' game against the Boilermakers in the NCAA Tournament Sweet 16. The game will feature the NCAA's all-time assists leader in Purdue's Braden Smith.Miller knows what it takes to be a great point guard. He ranks second in Pitt history for total assists after leading the Panthers to three NCAA Tournament appearances. He's developed players at the position, too, including Indiana Pacers point guard T.J. McConnell when Miller coached at Arizona.Now, Miller is in preparation to coach against one of the most accomplished point guards in college basketball in Smith. The Longhorns' coach was not shy to acknowledge Smith's accomplishments.\"Braden Smith, the only statistic you need to know about him if your from my generation, is you know if you break Bobby Hurley's assist record,\" Miller said, then continued. \"To break that record, I mean think about it. It's been since what, 1992? That's a long run.\"Smith broke Bobby Hurley's longstanding NCAA assist record during the Boilermakers first-round game in the tournament against Queens. Smith, a Westfield native, has become a stalwart in Purdue's offense for the last four years, and has been among the best guards in college basketball.What makes him so great? Miller dove into that as well.\"Incredible vision, incredible command of the game. Everybody likes their point guard to make the game easier for his teammates, and there's no doubt,\" he said. \"Like, that's his greatest gift. Everybody at Purdue benefits by having him on the court. He's incredible.\"Will Miller's Longhorns be able to slow him down? We'll find out in a short time when the teams tip off at 7:10 p.m. Thursday on CBS.Nathan Baird and Sam King have the best Purdue sports coverage, and sign up for IndyStar's Boilermakers newsletter.",
